{"key":"apphy2-mcq-025","kind":"mcq","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"C","final_answer":"may be nonzero","working":"Electric field is the negative gradient of potential, E = -dV/dx. The value of the potential at a single point does not determine the field there; the field depends on how potential changes with position. A point can have V = 0 while nearby potential differs, giving a nonzero field. For example, at the midpoint between equal and opposite point charges, the potentials cancel to zero, but the electric field is nonzero and points from the positive charge toward the negative charge.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":404,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":148,"cacheReadTokens":256},"outputTokens":512,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":129,"reasoningTokens":383},"totalTokens":916,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":404,"completion_tokens":512,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":256,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":148,"total_tokens":916,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":256},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":383}},"reasoningTokens":383,"cachedInputTokens":256},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":6011,"keyed_label":"C"}
